          "coach": {
            "eyebrow": "Decision · history",
            "title": "You have one focused question first. Make it count.",
            "body": "You will take a full history, but the first question should be the one that could change your plan fastest."
          },
          "question": {
            "prompt": "What do you ask first?",
            "options": [
              { "text": "Screen for danger signs — vomiting everything, convulsions, confusion, inability to drink", "correct": true },
              { "text": "Ask about her occupation and home address", "correct": false },
              { "text": "Ask which medicines she likes to take", "correct": false },
              { "text": "Ask about family history of headache", "correct": false }
            ],
            "right": "In a febrile adult, danger signs decide the setting of care — manage here, or stabilise and refer. Everything else can follow once you know she is safe to treat as an outpatient.",
            "wrong": "These can wait. With fever and headache, your first question should rule danger signs in or out — they decide whether she stays or is referred. The others don’t change your immediate plan."
          }
        },
        {
          "kind": "decision",
          "chart": {
            "tag": "ORDERS",
            "sections": [
              {
                "type": "assessment",
                "title": "Assessment",
                "text": "Febrile illness, no danger signs.",
                "emphasis": "Suspect uncomplicated malaria."
              },
              {
                "type": "investigations",
                "title": "Investigations",
                "orders": [
                  { "name": "Malaria RDT", "sub": "Not ordered", "reveal_sub": "SENT 09:54 · LAB-2271", "status": "order", "reveal_status": "pending" },
                  { "name": "Random blood sugar", "status": "order" },
                  { "name": "Urinalysis", "status": "order" }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "coach": {
            "eyebrow": "Decision · investigation",
            "title": "No danger signs. She’s safe to manage here. Next?",
            "body": "History is reassuring: she is drinking, not vomiting, fully alert, no neck stiffness, no recent travel. Malaria is high on your list."
          },
          "question": {
            "prompt": "How do you proceed?",
            "options": [
              { "text": "Order a malaria RDT and wait for the result before treating", "correct": true },
              { "text": "Start antimalarials now — it’s almost certainly malaria", "correct": false },
              { "text": "Give a broad-spectrum antibiotic to be safe", "correct": false },
              { "text": "Send her home with paracetamol and review in 3 days", "correct": false }
            ],
            "right": "Test before you treat. Uganda Clinical Guidelines require a parasitological diagnosis (RDT or microscopy) before antimalarials in all age groups where testing is available. It also protects against missing a non-malarial cause.",
            "wrong": "Presumptive treatment is exactly what the test-before-treat rule exists to stop. Order the RDT first — antimalarials without a positive test waste medicine and can mask another diagnosis. Antibiotics aren’t indicated yet, and sending her home misses a treatable cause."
          }
        },
        {
          "kind": "story",
